    </seriesStmt><sourceDesc><p>Born digital.</p></sourceDesc></fileDesc><revisionDesc><change who="http://syriaca.org/documentation/editors.xml#autogenerated" when="2026-07-28-05:00">CREATED: This bibl record was autogenerated from a Zotero record.</change><change who="https://www.zotero.org/cbss_admin" when="2022-11-18T14:33:45Z">Record created in Zotero.</change></revisionDesc></teiHeader><text><body><biblStruct type="journalArticle"><analytic><author><forename>Cyril</forename><surname>Moss</surname></author><title level="a">[Deprecated] Amphilochius of Iconium on John 14, 28: “The Father Who Sent Me, Is Greater Than I”</title><idno type="URI">http://syriaca.org/cbss/N5LESKY5</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/a_comprehensive_bibliography_on_syriac_studies/items/N5LESKY5</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/4861694/items/N5LESKY5</idno></analytic><monogr><title level="j">Le Muséon</title><imprint><date>1930</date></imprint><biblScope unit="vol">43</biblScope><biblScope unit="pp">317-364</biblScope></monogr><note type="abstract">"The Homily ["Oratio in illud: "Quia pater maior me est"" (Ioh. 14, 28) ("uersio syriaca")] is an attack on the Arians. These heretics asserted on the basis of such Texts as John, 14,28. : "My Father is greated than I", that Christ is manifestly inferior to the Father. Amphilochius energetically combats their position. In the first part of his sermon he shows on logical and philosophical grounds that it is impossible for a man rightly to maintain that God the Son is less than the Father, such an assertion being a derogation of the Father's honour and not simply an attack on the Son. [...] The second part of the discourse is devoted to a refutation of the Arian position from Scripture. In this section we have inter alia, a detailed exposition of John, 14, 23-15, 1." Three biblical passages quoted in the Homily: Samuel I, 9,2 ; Wisdom 1,7; Ephesians 2,14.</note></biblStruct><bibl type="formatted" subtype="bibliography" resp="https://www.zotero.org/styles/chicago-note-bibliography-17th-edition">
  Moss, Cyril. “[Deprecated] Amphilochius of Iconium on John 14, 28: ‘The Father Who Sent Me, Is Greater Than I.’” <title level="m">Le Muséon</title> 43 (1930): 317–64.
